WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.780
Every test corpus of any size has the same problem.

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.980
You can run everything, or you can run one thing.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:12.320
Running exactly the right subset, and proving later which subset that was, is the awkward part.

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:13.920
Tags help.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:19.760
Until the tag query lives in a CI job parameter that nobody can reconstruct three weeks later.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:27.020
runcomposer makes the selection a document, a run spec, the filter, kept for provenance,

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:32.640
the exact list of tests, embedded, and the hash of the catalog it was cut from.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:34.820
Then hand it to anything.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:36.560
A process pool here.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.000
A parameterized CI job.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:44.480
Or one small standard library Python file on a machine run Composer cannot even reach,

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:47.220
running your own command against exactly those IDs.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:50.000
Results come back however you like.

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:51.940
An HTTP push.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:53.300
A watch directory.

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:55.420
A bundle you commit to a branch.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:01:00.140
The marker inside carries the run ID, so the transport is your business.

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:03.920
The interface is a document, not a protocol.

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:05.740
runcomposer.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:06.980
Open Source.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:07.680
MIT.
